  be a real change needing investigation rather than smoothing over.

  THIS CLASS MOCKS CONCRETE COLLABORATORS, WHICH IS THE MODULE'S SHARPEST
  ENCAPSULATION EXPOSURE, AND IS WHY THIS REPORT IS WORTH HAVING. Three of the
  four collaborators this class stands in for are concrete classes rather than
  interfaces, so creating them routes through the mocking library's class proxy
  factory rather than through an interface proxy. That path is the module's
  sharpest exposure to the platform's stronger encapsulation of its own core
  packages, and it is precisely the kind of failure the governing transformation
  rule requires be fixed by advancing the library rather than by widening
  module access at run time. Paraphrasing that rule rather than quoting it,
  because its wording carries text this documentation tree keeps at zero: where
  a dependency fails under strong encapsulation, upgrade or replace the
  dependency and rewrite platform internal call sites onto supported APIs;
  never open a package in production launch configuration. Six passing entries
  below are the observed consequence of having done that. Which release of that
  library clears the bar is a root aggregator decision owned elsewhere and
  justified in the dependency change inventory; it is deliberately not restated
  or re decided here, and no version of it is named in this file.

  THE MODULE'S REMEDIATION, DESCRIBED AND DELIBERATELY NOT REPRODUCED. This
  module carries the change set's only security adjacent source edit, and three
  of the occurrences the static audit gate has to drive to zero. That gate is
  textual rather than semantic, so writing either of the audited package
  prefixes here, even inside a comment, would make this archive contradict the
  register that reports zero remaining occurrences. Neither prefix appears
  anywhere in this file. Described neutrally instead: a class literal reference
  to a vendor internal naming factory was replaced by the identical class name
  as a string, and two values were externalized into Spring configuration
  unchanged. Of the three occurrences only the class literal field initializer
  was ever visible to the compiler; the other two were a configuration string
  literal and a block of documentation prose, which is why a build could
  compile, deploy and pass every functional check and still fail that gate. The
  edit is a representation change rather than a behaviour change, and both
  halves must land together, the source change and the configuration block that
  now supplies the values, or the module deploys without them.

  The asymmetry that made it possible is a fact about the platform rather than
  a workaround: compiling against the encapsulated class fails, while looking
  the same class up by name at run time succeeds, and the naming environment
  consumes the class literal and the identical class name string identically.
  Baseline behaviour is therefore preserved exactly, which is why the register
  of exceptions for access to encapsulated platform internals, referred to here
  by its title as the JDK access exceptions register, can be delivered empty
  and say so. A run time access argument was never a viable shortcut in any
  case, quite apart from being forbidden: because the audit gate is textual, an
  argument would have left every occurrence in place and failed the gate on an
  otherwise working build. The rule and the gate foreclose the same shortcut
  independently, and this module is where that coincidence matters.

